WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_otl_111'), 'The API dashboard during the incident, from the histogram http.server.request.duration with the attributes http.request.method, http.route and http.response.status_code:

POST /submissions   19:30-19:40   p50 0.11 s   p95 0.18 s   p99 0.31 s   5xx 0.1%
POST /submissions   19:40-19:50   p50 0.14 s   p95 2.6 s    p99 3.1 s    5xx 4.2%

Statement: "This histogram is enough to find which submission requests were slow and in which step - database, queue or grading service - the 2.5 extra seconds were spent."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. A metric is an aggregate: the histogram counts requests per duration bucket for each combination of method, route and status. It tells you that something is wrong and how much (p95 went from 0.18 s to 2.6 s, 4.2% of responses are 5xx) and when it started, which is exactly what an alert needs. It has no request ids and no breakdown into steps, and adding a submission id or a step name as an attribute would turn one time series into millions (lesson 8). To see where the time went inside one slow request you need its trace: the spans of the database call, the enqueue and the HTTP call to grading-service, each with its own duration. Many backends link the two with exemplars, sample trace ids stored next to a histogram bucket. The misconception is that a detailed enough dashboard can replace traces; it can tell you which route, never which request or which step.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_otl_111') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
