    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_otl_111'), 'To cut the tracing bill, the team changes the API''s OpenTelemetry setup (simplified):

const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  sampler: new ParentBasedSampler({ root: new TraceIdRatioBasedSampler(0.1) }),
  traceExporter: new OTLPTraceExporter(),
  metricReader: new PeriodicExportingMetricReader({ exporter: new OTLPMetricExporter() }),
  instrumentations: [getNodeAutoInstrumentations()],
});

The dashboard''s request rate and error rate come from the http.server.request.duration histogram that the HTTP instrumentation records through the meter, not from spans.

Statement: "After the change, about 90% of new traces are no longer recorded, but the dashboard''s request count and error rate stay accurate."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The sampler decides which traces are recorded and exported; it does not touch metric instruments. The HTTP instrumentation records the duration histogram for every request, sampled or not, so request counts, error rates and percentiles still cover 100% of the traffic. With ParentBasedSampler, requests that arrive with a parent keep the parent''s decision, so roughly 10% of new root traces are kept. This is why metrics are the right source for alerts and rates, and traces for examples of individual requests. The misconception is that sampling traces makes every signal approximate; it only does when the metrics are computed from spans afterwards, for example by a collector component that turns received spans into metrics - then they only see the sampled 10%.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_otl_111') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
