WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_otl_121'), 'The API''s pino logger and a call in the submissions controller:

const logger = pino({
  level: "info",
  redact: ["authorization", "password"],
});

logger.info({ req: { method: req.method, url: req.url, headers: req.headers } }, "submission received");

// req.headers = { authorization: "Bearer eyJhbGciOi...", "content-type": "application/json" }

Statement: "With this config the bearer token in req.headers.authorization is replaced by [Redacted] in the written line."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. pino''s redact option takes paths, not key names to look for anywhere: "authorization" matches only a top-level key called authorization. The token sits at req.headers.authorization, so it is written in full. The path has to be spelled out ("req.headers.authorization") or use a wildcard ("*.headers.authorization"); check the pino docs for the path syntax of your version. The safer habit is to not log whole header objects at all: pick the fields you need. The misconception is that a redact list works like a search for a key name at any depth; in pino it does not (the logger you write in this lesson''s exercise does, by design), and a test that logs a nested secret is the only way to know which kind you have.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_otl_121') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
