    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_otl_211'), 'Code in the API process (the OpenTelemetry SDK with the AsyncLocalStorage context manager is started before the app):
// submissions.service.ts, inside the handler of POST /submissions, after the insert
this.scanner.once(`scanned:${submission.id}`, (verdict: string) => {
  this.logger.info({ verdict, submission_id: submission.id }, "scan result");
});

// antivirus.poller.ts, started once at boot
setInterval(() => tracer.startActiveSpan("antivirus.poll", async (span) => {
  for (const r of await this.scannerApi.results()) this.scanner.emit(`scanned:${r.submissionId}`, r.verdict);
  span.end();
}), 250);

Log line:
{"msg":"scan result","verdict":"clean","submission_id":"sub_7c1","trace_id":"9e01c3b5a7d24f6e8b1a0c2d4e6f8a1b"}
(9e01c3b5... is the trace of an antivirus.poll span; the request''s trace is 4bf92f35...)

Statement: "The listener logs the poller''s trace id because emit() calls it synchronously, in the context active inside emit(), not in the context that was active when once() registered it."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. An EventEmitter keeps its listeners in a list and calls them synchronously from emit(). AsyncLocalStorage only restores a context for async work that Node itself created (promises, timers, I/O); a plain function call inherits whatever is active, here the antivirus.poll span the poller started. Registering the listener inside the request does not capture anything. The fix is to bind the listener where it is registered: context.bind(context.active(), listener). The common misconception is that AsyncLocalStorage makes every callback remember where it was created; it does so only for async resources, not for callbacks stored in a library''s own data structures.', 1, 5
