The stored job, read from Redis:
{"name":"grade","data":{"submissionId":"sub_7c1"},"opts":{"attempts":3}}

Worker process, started with: node --import ./otel.js dist/worker.js
(otel.js starts the same SDK and context manager as the API.)
new Worker("grading", (job) => tracer.startActiveSpan("process grading", { kind: SpanKind.CONSUMER }, async (span) => { ... }));

Statement: "Both processes run the SDK with the AsyncLocalStorage context manager, so the process grading span already continues the request''s trace; the separate root trace the backend shows must be a display problem of the backend."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. AsyncLocalStorage lives in the memory of one process. The only thing that reaches the worker is the job, and the stored job contains just the submission id: no traceparent, no carrier of any kind. With nothing to extract, the worker''s startActiveSpan has no parent and the SDK correctly starts a new trace. The fix is to inject the active context into the job data in the producer (propagation.inject(context.active(), carrier)) and extract it in the worker (propagation.extract(ROOT_CONTEXT, job.data.otel)). The misconception is to treat "the SDK runs everywhere" as "the context flows everywhere"; across a process it flows only inside the message, unless an instrumentation for the queue library writes it there for you, which this job shows did not happen.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_otl_211') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
