Evidence D - the code added last Thursday:
async create(dto, user) {
  await this.auth.verify(user);
  const id = this.validate(dto);
  const problem = await this.cache.get(`problem:${dto.problemId}`);
  this.logger.info({ submission_id: id }, "calling plagiarism service");
  const verdict = await this.checkPlagiarism(id, dto.code);
  await this.repo.insert({ id, userId: user.id, problemId: problem.id, verdict });
  await this.enqueueGrading(id);
  return { id };
}
private checkPlagiarism(submissionId, code) {
  const startTime = Date.now();
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    // callback client, one connection opened at startup
    this.plagiarism.check({ code }, (err, result) => {
      const span = tracer.startSpan("plagiarism.check", { startTime, attributes: { "submission.id": submissionId } });
      span.end();
      this.logger.info({ submission_id: submissionId, verdict: result?.verdict, took_ms: Date.now() - startTime }, "plagiarism verdict");
      err ? reject(err) : resolve(result.verdict);
    });
  });
}

Statement: "The same lost context also detaches the work after the call, so the fix must wrap repo.insert and enqueueGrading in context.bind as well, not only the plagiarism callback."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. A shows INSERT submissions and send grading as children of POST /submissions, in the right trace. They run after await this.checkPlagiarism(...), and an await continuation resumes in the context of the async function that awaited, whoever resolved the promise. Only the code inside the callback runs in the context of the client''s connection, which was opened at startup with no span active. The fix belongs where the context is lost: bind the callback passed to check (or start the span before the call). The misconception is to spread context.bind over code that never lost its context, which hides where the real boundary is.', 5, 5
