Last Sunday the grader was fully down from 02:00 to 03:30 (90 minutes, every submit request failed).
Traffic: 28-day average 307 valid requests/min; Sunday 02:00-03:30: 60 valid requests/min; weekday peak about 2,000 valid requests/min.
Valid requests in the 28 days: 12,400,000.

Statement: "The outage used 90 of the 201.6 minutes, so it consumed about 45% of the error budget."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The SLI counts requests, so the budget is 0.5% x 12,400,000 = 62,000 bad requests. The outage failed 90 x 60 = 5,400 requests, about 8.7% of the budget. The 201.6-minute figure is the budget translated into time at the average rate of 307 requests per minute; at night traffic was a fifth of that, so each minute of outage spent a fifth as much. The same 90 minutes at the 2,000-requests-per-minute peak would have failed 180,000 requests, nearly three times the whole budget. The misconception is that "minutes of downtime" is what a request-based SLO measures: it is only a way to talk about it.', 3, 5
